## 7. Periodic processes & the Pacemaker Law

Anything recurring is a **scheduled pacemaker job**, never a habit and never a note in
someone's head. Language models are conversational and stop after each turn; without an automated
heartbeat trigger, periodic processes halt the moment human attention leaves.

The binding names the mechanism; the requirements are:

1. **Every process owner has a pacemaker.** Every agent role that owns a recurring process
   in `docs/processes.md` must have an active scheduled job configured to wake it.
2. **One job, one thin trigger.** A job notifies the owning persistent session UUID; the work
   runs in that session, under the current law.
3. **Never put procedure in a job's payload.** A job's definition cannot be
   updated by a change to this file, so procedure placed inside one silently
   goes stale while the law moves on.
4. **Deliver directly to the owning session UUID**, never to the chat root or via relay hops.
5. **Creating a job is a write, and a write needs a receipt.** Never announce a
   scheduled job without the tool result that created it.
6. **Resolve a job's owner before you touch it, and name your own jobs after your factory.**
   Before disabling, deleting, editing or repacing any cron row, resolve its owner through
   the registry attribution (`tools/registry.py resolve`, `docs/factory-registry.md`); a job
   you cannot attribute to your own factory is **reported, never edited**.
   *Cron names are namespaced per factory. A factory's own jobs are named after its
   **declared prefix** — its `job_prefixes` entry — and never after its slug:
   `{{FACTORY_SLUG}}` names the factory, while its `job_prefixes` entry names its jobs,
   and the two may differ. The manifest's `job_prefixes` is that law made
   mechanical: the prefixes are the only reason a job on a shared box can be attributed to
   its owner at all, so no two factories may claim the same prefix. Never disable, delete,
   edit or repace a job that is attributed to another factory — a job you cannot attribute
   is not yours to touch, and it is reported instead. The naming half is checkable per
   factory, because a factory can read its own jobs; the edit ban is not, because it governs
   a cron table no single factory owns, and its upholding mechanism is the process above
   rather than a gate (#101).*

